;; FH-EVENT-002 — one event or nil, and the closed listener options.
;;
;; After the shallow listener options are interpreted, an event-producing
;; site yields exactly ONE event vector or `nil`. `nil` dispatches
;; nothing; a vector of event vectors is the multi-intent mistake and is
;; rejected. The options map is closed: `:event` plus five listener
;; options, so a typo is a loud reject rather than an option that
;; silently does nothing.
;;
;; A MAP at an event position is that options map and NOTHING else
;; (D007, discharged DELETE). So a string-keyed map is an unknown-option
;; reject like any other typo, and an empty map is an options map with no
;; `:event` — neither is quietly re-read as some second grammar.
;;
;; MEMBERSHIP IS NOT THE WHOLE LAW. `:passive` and `:prevent-default` are
;; both roster members and they contradict each other: a passive listener
;; promises the browser it will never call `preventDefault`, so authoring
;; both asks for a mechanic the platform is entitled to ignore and one of
;; the two options silently does nothing — the exact failure the closed
;; roster exists to remove, in the one shape a roster cannot see. The
;; compiled tier always refused it and said "in any stage"; the plan is what
;; the mounted and structural tiers ask, so it refuses it too (rf2-uvcm3).
;; The two accepted `:options-every-…` rows are therefore a PAIR — one per
;; side of the native-attachment lane — and between them they carry all six
;; roster keys, so nothing is lost by there being no single legal map that
;; carries every one at once.
{:fh/id "FH-EVENT-002"
 :fh/law
 "An event-producing site yields exactly one event vector or nil after the closed listener options are interpreted; a map at an event position is the listener-options map and nothing else, so an unknown option key (a string key included), a mutually contradictory pair of roster options, or a multi-intent vector is rejected."

 ;; `:form` names a value the suite constructs; `:plan` is the normalized
 ;; site plan both emitters and the structural host read.
 :plans
 [{:form :event-vector
   :plan {:role :event-vector :event [:cart/add 7]}}
  {:form :options-plain
   :plan {:role :event-options :event [:article/save 3]}}
  {:form :options-prevent-default
   :plan {:role :event-options :event [:article/save 3] :prevent-default true}}
  {:note "every option that can co-exist, on the preventDefault side of the lane"
   :form :options-every-blocking-option
   :plan {:role :event-options :event [:picker/move 1]
          :prevent-default true :stop-propagation true
          :once true :capture true}}
  {:note "and on the passive side — between them the two rows carry all six roster keys"
   :form :options-every-passive-option
   :plan {:role :event-options :event [:picker/move 1]
          :stop-propagation true :once true :passive true :capture true}}
  {:form :options-false-options
   :plan {:role :event-options :event [:article/save 3]}}
  {:form :nil
   :plan nil}]

 :rejected-plans
 [{:form :options-unknown-key   :error-id :rf.error/view-bad-event}
  {:form :options-no-event      :error-id :rf.error/view-bad-event}
  {:form :options-event-not-vector :error-id :rf.error/view-bad-event}
  {:note "a string-keyed map is an unknown option, never a second grammar"
   :form :options-string-key    :error-id :rf.error/view-bad-event}
  {:note "an empty map is an options map missing its :event"
   :form :options-empty         :error-id :rf.error/view-bad-event}
  {:note "both keys are IN the roster and contradict each other — a passive listener promises never to preventDefault, so one of the two would silently do nothing"
   :form :options-passive-prevent-default
   :error-id :rf.error/view-bad-event}
  {:form :string                :error-id :rf.error/view-bad-event}
  {:form :number                :error-id :rf.error/view-bad-event}
  {:form :keyword               :error-id :rf.error/view-bad-event}]

 ;; What a committed site actually dispatches when it fires. `:returns`
 ;; is what a `v/event` body yields.
 :outcomes
 [{:returns [:picker/accept]              :dispatched [[:picker/accept]]}
  {:returns [:picker/move 1]              :dispatched [[:picker/move 1]]}
  {:returns nil                           :dispatched []}]

 :rejected-outcomes
 [{:returns :picker/accept                :error-id :rf.error/view-bad-event}
  {:returns "picker/accept"               :error-id :rf.error/view-bad-event}
  {:returns [[:picker/accept] [:picker/close]] :error-id :rf.error/view-bad-event}]

 ;; `:once` is site state, retained across commits while the site stays
 ;; mounted: three firings, one dispatch.
 :once {:firings 3 :dispatched [[:boot/ready]]}

 :recovery :yield-one-event-vector-or-nil}
